1
00:00:01,496 --> 00:00:04,406
Hello everybody, and thank
you for joining me today.

2
00:00:05,186 --> 00:00:07,696
I want to start with a
confession about our profession.

3
00:00:08,706 --> 00:00:12,905
For most of our careers, the
worst thing a broken data pipeline

4
00:00:12,906 --> 00:00:14,845
could do was embarrass us.

5
00:00:15,535 --> 00:00:19,265
A wrong number shows up on a
dashboard, someone notices it on

6
00:00:19,265 --> 00:00:23,745
a Monday morning meeting, we trace
it, we fix it, and we move on.

7
00:00:24,515 --> 00:00:28,855
The data was wrong, but a human
was always standing between the

8
00:00:28,865 --> 00:00:31,216
da-data and any real decision.

9
00:00:32,185 --> 00:00:37,065
That era is ending, and almost
nobody has redesigned their

10
00:00:37,085 --> 00:00:39,095
data layer for what comes next.

11
00:00:40,675 --> 00:00:43,896
A quick word on who I am,
and then we get into it.

12
00:00:45,056 --> 00:00:49,465
I am Vishwak Tadikonda, a senior
architect with SDH Systems.

13
00:00:50,346 --> 00:00:54,755
For about twelve years, I have
built large-scale systems across

14
00:00:54,775 --> 00:00:57,025
retail, fintech, and mobility.

15
00:00:58,045 --> 00:01:00,665
The numbers on this
slide are the shorthand.

16
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:06,315
Platforms spanning tens of thousands
of locations, infrastructure

17
00:01:06,325 --> 00:01:08,616
handling millions of transactions.

18
00:01:09,375 --> 00:01:15,685
The work behind this talk is enterprise
master data, the systems that decide

19
00:01:15,755 --> 00:01:21,955
what a customer is, what a store is, what
a product is across an entire company.

20
00:01:22,785 --> 00:01:24,536
I'm not gonna read you my resume.

21
00:01:25,056 --> 00:01:27,835
I would rather earn your
attention with the content.

22
00:01:28,405 --> 00:01:32,625
So here is what the next half
hour looks like Four parts.

23
00:01:33,265 --> 00:01:39,455
First, the shift happening right
now from AI that observes to AI

24
00:01:39,505 --> 00:01:45,485
that acts, and the gap it opens in
the data layer most of us built.

25
00:01:46,455 --> 00:01:47,955
Second, the fix.

26
00:01:48,635 --> 00:01:52,105
The four pillars of
action-grade master data.

27
00:01:52,925 --> 00:01:58,035
How real-time resolution actually
works and the stack it runs on.

28
00:01:59,065 --> 00:02:03,515
Third, operating it in
production, which is where drift,

29
00:02:03,635 --> 00:02:05,985
observability, and safety live.

30
00:02:06,815 --> 00:02:12,795
And fourth, a live demo where you
watch the failure and the fix side

31
00:02:12,805 --> 00:02:19,165
by side, the traps to avoid, and a
phased roadmap you can start on Monday.

32
00:02:20,905 --> 00:02:24,015
The title is From Analytics to Action.

33
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:29,395
That shift from analytics to
action is the whole ballgame.

34
00:02:30,135 --> 00:02:31,295
Let me show you what I mean

35
00:02:32,295 --> 00:02:37,155
Think about how our relationship
with data has evolved in three acts.

36
00:02:38,195 --> 00:02:40,115
Act one was observe.

37
00:02:40,815 --> 00:02:42,235
We built dashboards.

38
00:02:42,845 --> 00:02:45,405
Data told us what happened yesterday.

39
00:02:46,105 --> 00:02:48,765
A human looked at it
and drew a conclusion.

40
00:02:49,665 --> 00:02:51,635
Act two was recommend.

41
00:02:52,345 --> 00:02:55,035
The models got good enough to suggest.

42
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:58,245
Here is a customer likely to churn.

43
00:02:58,795 --> 00:03:00,735
Here is a product to reorder.

44
00:03:01,385 --> 00:03:03,625
But a human still made the call.

45
00:03:04,875 --> 00:03:09,505
Act three is act, and
that is not a forecast.

46
00:03:10,025 --> 00:03:11,135
It's already here.

47
00:03:12,265 --> 00:03:14,205
Agents are placing the reorder.

48
00:03:15,065 --> 00:03:17,635
Agents are issuing the retention offer.

49
00:03:18,415 --> 00:03:22,935
Agents are re-triggering the
workflow directly with no

50
00:03:22,955 --> 00:03:25,235
human reviewing each decision.

51
00:03:26,735 --> 00:03:31,145
Here's the part that should make
every architect watching this sit up.

52
00:03:32,155 --> 00:03:37,245
In act one and act two, bad
data produced bad information,

53
00:03:37,815 --> 00:03:39,625
and a human could catch it.

54
00:03:40,235 --> 00:03:45,105
In act three, bad data produces
a bad action that has already

55
00:03:45,105 --> 00:03:48,185
happened by the time anyone looks.

56
00:03:48,775 --> 00:03:50,745
So the question becomes unavoidable.

57
00:03:51,595 --> 00:03:56,605
What breaks when we point an
autonomous agent at a data layer

58
00:03:56,875 --> 00:03:58,885
that was never designed for this?

59
00:03:59,885 --> 00:04:04,475
The honest answer is
almost everything, quietly.

60
00:04:05,705 --> 00:04:07,055
Look at what we actually built.

61
00:04:07,955 --> 00:04:11,495
Our data stacks were
optimized for analytics.

62
00:04:12,075 --> 00:04:14,655
Batch pipelines that run overnight.

63
00:04:15,315 --> 00:04:19,605
Eventual consistency, because a
report can wait a few minutes.

64
00:04:20,445 --> 00:04:24,765
Fuzzy entity matching that is good
enough for a quarterly roll-up.

65
00:04:25,715 --> 00:04:30,425
Every one of those choices was
correct for a human reading a report,

66
00:04:30,955 --> 00:04:35,695
and every one of them is dangerous
for an agent taking an action.

67
00:04:36,805 --> 00:04:38,125
This is the gap.

68
00:04:38,905 --> 00:04:43,025
It is not that our data is
bad, it is that our data layer

69
00:04:43,085 --> 00:04:44,705
is solving the wrong problem.

70
00:04:45,605 --> 00:04:47,655
It was built to inform a person.

71
00:04:48,325 --> 00:04:52,255
We are now asking it to
govern a machine that acts.

72
00:04:53,085 --> 00:04:59,285
Stale records and unresolved entities
used to pro-produce a misleading chart.

73
00:04:59,895 --> 00:05:03,255
Now, they trigger a live
operational decision

74
00:05:04,255 --> 00:05:08,095
Let me make that concrete with
one that actually happened.

75
00:05:09,035 --> 00:05:11,995
I will keep it anonymized,
but the shape is real.

76
00:05:13,125 --> 00:05:19,075
A replenishment agent at one of our retail
deployments started placing duplicate

77
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:22,195
orders across a whole cluster of stores.

78
00:05:22,785 --> 00:05:28,515
Nothing had shipped, no new code,
no model change, nothing touched.

79
00:05:29,245 --> 00:05:32,445
The agent was behaving
exactly as designed.

80
00:05:33,275 --> 00:05:36,225
The cause was upstream, and it was tiny.

81
00:05:37,015 --> 00:05:42,185
Someone improved an address normalization
step, a good change on its own,

82
00:05:42,705 --> 00:05:47,685
but it caused one store to resolve
to two different golden records.

83
00:05:48,465 --> 00:05:54,056
And from the agent's point of view,
two golden records means two stores.

84
00:05:54,755 --> 00:05:58,065
So it ordered opening inventory for both.

85
00:05:59,055 --> 00:06:04,005
Every order looked completely
valid in isolation, which is

86
00:06:04,035 --> 00:06:06,225
exactly why nothing alarmed.

87
00:06:06,895 --> 00:06:08,185
It ran for two days.

88
00:06:08,945 --> 00:06:13,666
By the time we caught it, that was
ninety-four hundred duplicate orders

89
00:06:13,965 --> 00:06:15,846
and about forty-one thousand dollars.

90
00:06:16,555 --> 00:06:19,765
And here is the number that matters most.

91
00:06:20,415 --> 00:06:23,365
Zero lines of agent code were at fault.

92
00:06:24,006 --> 00:06:26,345
The agent did its job perfectly.

93
00:06:27,006 --> 00:06:28,386
The data lied to it.

94
00:06:29,885 --> 00:06:33,176
That is the whole thesis of
this talk in one incident.

95
00:06:33,725 --> 00:06:39,185
An agent is only ever as trustworthy
as the substrate underneath it.

96
00:06:40,235 --> 00:06:43,405
So let me name the ways
that substrate breaks

97
00:06:44,405 --> 00:06:47,505
There are four failure
modes I see again and again.

98
00:06:48,245 --> 00:06:52,525
One sentence on each, because we
are going to watch all four of

99
00:06:52,525 --> 00:06:54,985
them happen live in a few minutes.

100
00:06:55,795 --> 00:06:58,165
The first is state-- staleness.

101
00:06:58,925 --> 00:07:03,755
Your batch refresh means the agent
is acting on a picture of the world

102
00:07:04,075 --> 00:07:06,765
that is hours, sometimes days old.

103
00:07:07,345 --> 00:07:12,725
It is making confident decisions about
a reality that has already changed.

104
00:07:13,565 --> 00:07:17,505
The second is near-match identity errors.

105
00:07:18,365 --> 00:07:24,335
Fuzzy matching that was perfectly fine
for reporting now treats one entity

106
00:07:24,445 --> 00:07:27,465
as two, and the agent acts twice.

107
00:07:28,455 --> 00:07:31,475
The third is duplicate entities.

108
00:07:32,195 --> 00:07:37,145
When your golden record is not
truly resolved, the agent fires

109
00:07:37,145 --> 00:07:41,375
conflicting actions against what
it thinks are two different things,

110
00:07:41,995 --> 00:07:43,665
but are actually the same thing.

111
00:07:44,615 --> 00:07:46,305
The fourth is drift.

112
00:07:46,825 --> 00:07:49,795
Your schemas and your values drift slowly.

113
00:07:50,645 --> 00:07:54,595
No error is ever thrown,
and your resolution accuracy

114
00:07:55,135 --> 00:07:57,095
quietly rots from the inside.

115
00:07:57,915 --> 00:08:01,905
Each of these maps to a design
principle that fixes it.

116
00:08:02,715 --> 00:08:04,105
Here are the four principles

117
00:08:05,105 --> 00:08:08,285
If you remember nothing else
from this architecture part of

118
00:08:08,285 --> 00:08:10,235
this talk, remember these four.

119
00:08:10,975 --> 00:08:12,165
Immutability.

120
00:08:12,515 --> 00:08:14,945
Stop overriding records.

121
00:08:15,475 --> 00:08:16,115
Append.

122
00:08:16,715 --> 00:08:22,085
Keep the full history, so you can
always reconstruct exactly what the

123
00:08:22,085 --> 00:08:25,025
agent saw at the moment it decided.

124
00:08:25,765 --> 00:08:30,305
When something goes wrong, and
it will, you need that lineage.

125
00:08:31,705 --> 00:08:32,975
Version contracts.

126
00:08:33,585 --> 00:08:38,745
Every point where an agent
reads your data is an interface,

127
00:08:39,145 --> 00:08:41,455
and interfaces need contracts.

128
00:08:42,025 --> 00:08:48,495
Schema, allowed values, freshness
guarantees, all explicit, all versioned.

129
00:08:50,375 --> 00:08:51,855
Real-time resolution.

130
00:08:52,635 --> 00:08:57,375
Identity has to resolve in
milliseconds at the moment of that

131
00:08:57,375 --> 00:09:00,155
event, not overnight in a batch job.

132
00:09:01,095 --> 00:09:05,035
The agent cannot wait until
tomorrow to know who it is

133
00:09:05,035 --> 00:09:08,245
talking to Event-driven execution.

134
00:09:08,965 --> 00:09:14,445
State changes have to flow
reliably the instant they happen.

135
00:09:15,655 --> 00:09:20,655
Put those four together and you get
something our analytics stacks never

136
00:09:20,705 --> 00:09:28,135
had to be, a substrate an agent can
trust at the moment it acts, not just

137
00:09:28,155 --> 00:09:30,085
at the moment someone runs a query.

138
00:09:31,045 --> 00:09:32,845
Now let me ground this in something real

139
00:09:33,845 --> 00:09:36,775
I want to put a number on
the room we are operating in.

140
00:09:37,605 --> 00:09:43,615
The work behind the stock is a master
data platform for a global retailer with

141
00:09:43,615 --> 00:09:45,445
about thirty-eight thousand locations.

142
00:09:46,065 --> 00:09:51,145
I do this through SD8 systems, and at
that scale, you stop thinking about

143
00:09:51,195 --> 00:09:56,535
master data as a reporting asset that
lives in a warehouse, and you start

144
00:09:56,555 --> 00:10:02,295
treating it as a live operational layer
that the business runs on in real-time.

145
00:10:03,595 --> 00:10:05,745
The constraints are unforgiving.

146
00:10:06,805 --> 00:10:13,865
Tens of thousands of locations, a
target of resolving an entity end to

147
00:10:13,905 --> 00:10:20,515
end in under two hundred milliseconds,
and an absolute requirement that

148
00:10:20,585 --> 00:10:27,565
every action an agent takes happens
exactly once, no matter how many times

149
00:10:27,595 --> 00:10:30,215
the underlying event gets delivered.

150
00:10:31,535 --> 00:10:36,725
Those three numbers, thirty-eight
thousand, sub two hundred milliseconds,

151
00:10:36,885 --> 00:10:42,165
and exactly once, are the entire
engineering problem in a nutshell.

152
00:10:43,565 --> 00:10:47,385
Let me show you how the resolution
actually works under that kind of

153
00:10:47,395 --> 00:10:50,835
pressure This is the heart of it.

154
00:10:51,565 --> 00:10:58,245
The pipeline is ingest, normalize,
match, score, and publish, and

155
00:10:58,245 --> 00:11:02,285
the whole round trip has to
fit inside that latency budget.

156
00:11:03,165 --> 00:11:07,235
A few things make it
trustworthy, not just fast.

157
00:11:07,975 --> 00:11:09,715
We use deterministic keys.

158
00:11:10,275 --> 00:11:17,235
The same real-world entity always
computes to the same key, which means

159
00:11:17,515 --> 00:11:24,595
restarts, replace, and re-ingestion never
accidentally create a second copy of it.

160
00:11:25,595 --> 00:11:31,185
We score probab-probabilistically,
but we surface the confidence.

161
00:11:32,315 --> 00:11:34,595
The agent does not just get an answer.

162
00:11:35,285 --> 00:11:37,535
It gets how sure we are.

163
00:11:38,415 --> 00:11:40,445
High confidence, it acts.

164
00:11:41,025 --> 00:11:42,805
Borderline, it holds.

165
00:11:43,485 --> 00:11:49,245
That honesty is the real difference
between an agent that is safe and

166
00:11:49,245 --> 00:11:51,615
an agent that is merely confident.

167
00:11:53,525 --> 00:11:58,175
And the survive rules, the
logic that decides which value

168
00:11:58,195 --> 00:12:00,675
wins when sources disagree.

169
00:12:02,535 --> 00:12:08,105
Live in version control code,
sitting right next to the data

170
00:12:08,265 --> 00:12:14,635
contracts, not buried in some
pipeline config that nobody can find.

171
00:12:15,555 --> 00:12:17,795
The result is simple to state.

172
00:12:18,675 --> 00:12:24,255
The agent gets one trusted
entity fast with the confidence

173
00:12:24,345 --> 00:12:26,325
it can actually reason about

174
00:12:27,325 --> 00:12:30,045
I said resolution has to
happen in milliseconds.

175
00:12:30,775 --> 00:12:37,165
Let me open the hood on how, because the
how is where the real engineering lives.

176
00:12:38,125 --> 00:12:43,995
The naive way to resolve identity
is to compare each incoming record

177
00:12:44,105 --> 00:12:46,795
against every record you already have.

178
00:12:47,675 --> 00:12:50,025
That is fine for a thousand records.

179
00:12:50,585 --> 00:12:56,105
At our scale, it is billions of
comparisons per event, and you will

180
00:12:56,105 --> 00:12:58,035
never see two hundred milliseconds again.

181
00:12:59,525 --> 00:13:02,275
The trick that makes it
possible is blocking.

182
00:13:02,975 --> 00:13:08,895
Before any expensive comparison,
you generate a small candidate set.

183
00:13:10,105 --> 00:13:16,035
The handful of records this one could
plausibly match using cheap blocking keys.

184
00:13:16,745 --> 00:13:20,025
You turn a billion
comparisons into about five.

185
00:13:20,945 --> 00:13:26,125
Blocking is the unglamorous thing
that makes real-time resolution

186
00:13:26,235 --> 00:13:30,845
physically possible, and it
is the part most teams skip.

187
00:13:32,505 --> 00:13:39,635
Once you have candidates, the matching
combines signals, name, similarity,

188
00:13:40,225 --> 00:13:46,455
address geometry, geographic distance,
contact fields, each weighted,

189
00:13:47,035 --> 00:13:49,185
and the whole thing explainable.

190
00:13:49,835 --> 00:13:53,075
So you can defend why two records merged.

191
00:13:53,945 --> 00:13:56,395
And the output is not a yes or a no.

192
00:13:56,995 --> 00:13:58,435
It is a confidence band.

193
00:13:59,075 --> 00:14:01,845
Above a high floor, auto-merge.

194
00:14:02,455 --> 00:14:05,345
Below a low floor, treat as separate.

195
00:14:06,245 --> 00:14:08,345
In between, do not guess.

196
00:14:08,635 --> 00:14:10,025
Route to a human.

197
00:14:10,795 --> 00:14:15,215
The agent is always told
which band it is standing in.

198
00:14:16,875 --> 00:14:19,015
That is resolution under the hood.

199
00:14:20,225 --> 00:14:25,455
Now, the services it runs on
Quickly, the building blocks, because

200
00:14:25,465 --> 00:14:28,985
most of you know these services,
and I do not need to sell them.

201
00:14:29,705 --> 00:14:34,605
Lambda for the resolution itself,
stateless, event-triggered, scales

202
00:14:34,605 --> 00:14:40,555
with the firehose, and critically, it
reruns safely when an event is retried.

203
00:14:41,215 --> 00:14:46,305
DynamoDB as the golden record
store, single-digit millisecond

204
00:14:46,305 --> 00:14:50,935
reads, and its change streams are
the backbone of our data capture.

205
00:14:52,295 --> 00:14:57,775
EventBridge as the nervous system, a
schema-registered bus that routes the

206
00:14:57,775 --> 00:15:00,865
right entity changes to the right agents.

207
00:15:01,715 --> 00:15:03,165
None of these are exotic.

208
00:15:03,825 --> 00:15:07,375
The architecture is the
point, not the logos.

209
00:15:07,995 --> 00:15:14,205
Now I want to talk about the part everyone
underestimates until it burns them.

210
00:15:15,045 --> 00:15:18,075
What happens when the
same event shows up twice?

211
00:15:19,075 --> 00:15:23,745
In an event-driven sc- system at
scale, this is not an edge case.

212
00:15:24,155 --> 00:15:25,265
It is a guarantee.

213
00:15:25,845 --> 00:15:29,045
Networks retry, queues redeliver.

214
00:15:29,935 --> 00:15:35,265
The same event will arrive more
than once every single day.

215
00:15:36,495 --> 00:15:42,775
Idempotency means that no matter how
many times an event fires, the outcome

216
00:15:42,815 --> 00:15:46,305
is the same, as if it had fired once.

217
00:15:47,535 --> 00:15:50,575
Now here why this matters in act three.

218
00:15:51,155 --> 00:15:56,245
When the consumer was a dashboard, a
duplicate event was a rounding error.

219
00:15:57,075 --> 00:16:02,755
When the consumer is an agent, a duplicate
event is a second payment, a second

220
00:16:02,755 --> 00:16:06,045
shipment, a reward redeemed twice.

221
00:16:06,735 --> 00:16:09,595
The duplicate does not corrupt a number.

222
00:16:10,165 --> 00:16:13,005
It takes a real action in the world.

223
00:16:14,225 --> 00:16:16,595
We enforce it in three places.

224
00:16:17,365 --> 00:16:23,635
Deterministic request IDs at the moment
the event is born, conditional writes

225
00:16:23,675 --> 00:16:30,225
in DynamoDB that physically refuse to
commit the same transaction twice, and

226
00:16:30,245 --> 00:16:33,505
deduplication windows on the bus itself.

227
00:16:34,745 --> 00:16:36,995
I will say this as plainly as I can.

228
00:16:37,685 --> 00:16:42,825
Idempotency is the difference between
shipping one order and shipping

229
00:16:42,845 --> 00:16:48,655
three Once you have a trusted entity
change, it needs to reach everyone

230
00:16:48,665 --> 00:16:54,795
who cares about it without you
writing or wiring point-to-point

231
00:16:55,255 --> 00:16:57,605
spaghetti between every service.

232
00:16:58,495 --> 00:17:00,955
So we publish it once to the bus.

233
00:17:01,935 --> 00:17:06,675
The inventory agent subscribes,
the orchestration layer subscribes,

234
00:17:07,135 --> 00:17:09,065
the reward engine subscribes.

235
00:17:09,865 --> 00:17:14,065
Nobody pulls the golden record
store, and nobody calls it directly.

236
00:17:15,785 --> 00:17:18,625
Two things fall out of
this that you really want.

237
00:17:19,345 --> 00:17:25,895
Contracts get enforced at the routing
layer, so a malformed change never

238
00:17:25,905 --> 00:17:32,815
reaches a subscriber, and failures land
in a dead letter queue, which means you

239
00:17:32,815 --> 00:17:38,825
can replay them and you can reconstruct
exactly the sequence of events the

240
00:17:38,825 --> 00:17:40,945
agent saw when it made a decision.

241
00:17:42,005 --> 00:17:47,945
That replay capability is worth its
weight in gold the first time you have to

242
00:17:48,325 --> 00:17:50,685
debug an agent's behavior in production.

243
00:17:52,385 --> 00:17:56,865
But even with all of this in place,
there is one thing that degrades so

244
00:17:56,865 --> 00:18:02,855
slowly you will not notice until it
has already cost you: Drift is the

245
00:18:02,855 --> 00:18:05,525
silent killer of agentic systems.

246
00:18:06,195 --> 00:18:09,835
Because nothing breaks, no alarm goes off.

247
00:18:10,655 --> 00:18:15,775
The pipeline is green, and yet
your match quality is decaying

248
00:18:16,855 --> 00:18:19,115
a tenth of a percent at a time.

249
00:18:20,225 --> 00:18:24,325
Until one day, the agent is
acting on entities that are

250
00:18:24,345 --> 00:18:27,055
subtly, consistently wrong.

251
00:18:28,295 --> 00:18:31,705
You fight it by making
it visible three ways.

252
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:39,895
You define drift thresholds, match
confidence, field completeness, record

253
00:18:39,945 --> 00:18:43,425
age, and you alert the moment they move.

254
00:18:44,995 --> 00:18:46,625
You profile continuously.

255
00:18:47,265 --> 00:18:51,965
You watch the entity streams for
schema shifts and distribution shifts

256
00:18:52,395 --> 00:18:57,925
in real time instead of finding out
at the next audit And you close the

257
00:18:57,925 --> 00:18:59,915
loop with the agents themselves.

258
00:19:00,545 --> 00:19:04,035
When an agent's confidence
drops, that is a signal.

259
00:19:05,135 --> 00:19:07,875
Feed it back into the hub as a correction.

260
00:19:09,865 --> 00:19:14,125
The principle underneath all three
is the oldest one in operations.

261
00:19:15,095 --> 00:19:20,425
You cannot fix what you cannot see,
so make drift something you can see

262
