267
00:19:43,415 --> 00:19:54,077
And drift and recovery, the confidence
distribution, the record age, the

268
00:19:54,107 --> 00:19:59,717
idempotency rejections per hour, and
the replay success rate, which is the

269
00:19:59,717 --> 00:20:05,247
metric that proves you can actually
recover when something does go wrong.

270
00:20:06,367 --> 00:20:11,637
All of this rests on one thing
being stable underneath it, the

271
00:20:11,637 --> 00:20:15,917
contract This brings together
everything we've talked about.

272
00:20:16,477 --> 00:20:17,517
The data contract.

273
00:20:18,297 --> 00:20:21,317
Every agent binds to a version contract.

274
00:20:22,527 --> 00:20:28,047
It specifies the schema, the
allowed values, and the freshness

275
00:20:28,047 --> 00:20:29,757
guarantee it can rely on.

276
00:20:31,467 --> 00:20:35,377
If you want to make a breaking
change, you publish a new version.

277
00:20:35,867 --> 00:20:42,167
You do not silently mutate the one
that is alive, and any record that

278
00:20:42,197 --> 00:20:47,357
violates the contract is rejected
before it ever reaches an agent.

279
00:20:49,107 --> 00:20:54,667
The mental model I want to leave with
you is this: treat your data contract

280
00:20:54,727 --> 00:20:57,507
exactly like you treat an API contract.

281
00:20:58,797 --> 00:21:03,437
You would never let a service
silently change its API shape

282
00:21:03,537 --> 00:21:05,207
on consumers in production.

283
00:21:06,097 --> 00:21:08,027
Stop letting your data do it.

284
00:21:08,577 --> 00:21:14,767
Version it, enforce it at the bus,
and put a name on it, an owner,

285
00:21:15,047 --> 00:21:21,067
and a steward, so there is a human
accountable for that contract, not just

286
00:21:21,147 --> 00:21:24,417
a table everyone uses and no one owns.

287
00:21:25,587 --> 00:21:32,227
Now, contracts and the four guards stop
the failures we can name in advance,

288
00:21:32,707 --> 00:21:37,487
but a serious system also plans
for the failure it did not predict.

289
00:21:38,157 --> 00:21:40,987
So before the demo, one more layer.

290
00:21:41,557 --> 00:21:44,827
What happens when the
agent is wrong anyway?

291
00:21:45,827 --> 00:21:49,647
Everything so far has been
about preventing bad actions

292
00:21:49,857 --> 00:21:51,767
at the level of a single event.

293
00:21:52,577 --> 00:21:59,027
But you design a safe system assuming that
one day something gets through anyway.

294
00:22:00,027 --> 00:22:04,137
So you add a second kind of
defense, one that limits the blast

295
00:22:04,137 --> 00:22:06,617
radius of any single mistake.

296
00:22:07,227 --> 00:22:08,257
Four controls.

297
00:22:09,417 --> 00:22:10,447
Circuit breakers.

298
00:22:11,107 --> 00:22:13,747
These do not look at any one event.

299
00:22:14,147 --> 00:22:15,767
They watch the aggregate.

300
00:22:17,087 --> 00:22:21,647
If the rate of anomalies or low
confidence decisions suddenly spikes,

301
00:22:22,027 --> 00:22:24,627
the breaker trips and pauses the agent.

302
00:22:25,377 --> 00:22:29,977
Even though every individual
event looked fine, that is the one

303
00:22:29,987 --> 00:22:32,097
that saves you from a bad batch.

304
00:22:33,327 --> 00:22:34,327
Human in the loop.

305
00:22:34,907 --> 00:22:40,747
Above a certain impact or a certain
uncertainty, the action does not execute.

306
00:22:41,277 --> 00:22:42,947
It escalates to a person.

307
00:22:43,897 --> 00:22:49,887
You decide where that line sits,
but you draw it deliberately.

308
00:22:50,887 --> 00:22:51,747
A kill switch.

309
00:22:52,787 --> 00:22:59,187
One control that halts an agent
or a whole workflow instantly and

310
00:22:59,187 --> 00:23:04,467
preserves state, so you can replay
cleanly once you have found the cause.

311
00:23:06,177 --> 00:23:07,837
And blast radius limits.

312
00:23:08,467 --> 00:23:12,117
Rate caps, spend caps, segment fences.

313
00:23:13,057 --> 00:23:18,187
Hard ceilings on how much any
single decision is allowed to

314
00:23:18,187 --> 00:23:23,647
touch, so a bug stays an incident
instead of becoming a catastrophe.

315
00:23:24,777 --> 00:23:29,047
Now let me show you all of it,
the failures, the guards, and

316
00:23:29,047 --> 00:23:31,907
that last line of defense live

317
00:23:32,907 --> 00:23:37,787
What you're looking at is one
event stream feeding two systems.

318
00:23:38,417 --> 00:23:41,537
On the left, a legacy master data setup.

319
00:23:42,347 --> 00:23:44,897
Last write wins, fuzzy matching.

320
00:23:45,627 --> 00:23:49,737
On the right, the action grade
design we just walked through.

321
00:23:50,447 --> 00:23:54,797
The same autonomous agent
sits on top of both, acting

322
00:23:54,947 --> 00:23:57,897
on whatever each one resolves.

323
00:23:58,407 --> 00:23:59,367
What's the difference?

324
00:24:00,957 --> 00:24:03,377
First, a clean baseline.

325
00:24:03,997 --> 00:24:07,807
A new store comes online,
both lanes do the right thing.

326
00:24:08,357 --> 00:24:14,197
Good Now the same store arrives
again, different ID, messier address.

327
00:24:14,857 --> 00:24:15,857
Watch the left.

328
00:24:16,407 --> 00:24:23,397
Legacy treats it as a brand new store and
ships the opening inventory a second time.

329
00:24:24,397 --> 00:24:26,777
Eighteen thousand dollars gone.

330
00:24:27,587 --> 00:24:33,257
On the right, it resolves to the same
des- deterministic key and merges.

331
00:24:33,677 --> 00:24:38,377
No shipment A loyalty
reward retried by the bus.

332
00:24:39,187 --> 00:24:41,217
Same event twice.

333
00:24:42,017 --> 00:24:44,147
Legacy credits the member twice.

334
00:24:44,957 --> 00:24:51,387
The action grade lane sees the event ID is
already committed and rejects the entry.

335
00:24:51,987 --> 00:24:58,617
Exactly once holds A stale inventory
snapshot arrives late, out of order.

336
00:24:59,447 --> 00:25:06,527
Legacy overwrites good data with
old data and triggers an emergency

337
00:25:06,577 --> 00:25:08,757
restock the store never needed.

338
00:25:09,627 --> 00:25:15,647
The right lane checks the version,
sees it as stale, and refuses it Two

339
00:25:15,647 --> 00:25:18,157
different customers with similar names.

340
00:25:18,807 --> 00:25:26,187
Legacy auto merges them on a weak score
and hands one person's perks to another.

341
00:25:26,887 --> 00:25:32,647
The right lane sees the score is
in the uncertain band and routes

342
00:25:32,647 --> 00:25:39,737
it to a human instead of acting And
finally, an upstream service ships

343
00:25:39,737 --> 00:25:42,347
a record missing a required field.

344
00:25:43,197 --> 00:25:49,317
Legacy ingests it and routes a shipment
to a store that is actually closed.

345
00:25:49,977 --> 00:25:55,297
The write lane rejects it at the contract
and sends it to the dead letter queue.

346
00:25:55,767 --> 00:25:59,067
No harm Now, the last one.

347
00:25:59,957 --> 00:26:02,507
The safety net we just talked about.

348
00:26:03,087 --> 00:26:08,837
A bad upstream batch misfires and
fires a burst of low confidence

349
00:26:08,867 --> 00:26:13,607
merges, 120 of them all at once.

350
00:26:14,047 --> 00:26:15,007
Watch the left.

351
00:26:15,967 --> 00:26:23,397
Legacy has no concept of rate, so it just
keeps acting, acting, and number explodes.

352
00:26:24,207 --> 00:26:29,607
The right lane sees the rate cross
the rest-- the threshold, trips the

353
00:26:29,607 --> 00:26:32,207
circuit breaker, and pauses the agent.

354
00:26:32,807 --> 00:26:36,147
The whole backlog goes
to a human for review.

355
00:26:36,797 --> 00:26:37,527
Still zero.

356
00:26:39,107 --> 00:26:41,547
Same event, same agent.

357
00:26:41,697 --> 00:26:49,527
The legacy lane just took a hundred
125 harmful actions, over $120,000.

358
00:26:50,387 --> 00:26:53,267
The Action Grade lane took zero.

359
00:26:54,657 --> 00:26:58,217
The only thing that changed
is the data layer underneath

360
00:26:59,217 --> 00:27:03,317
Before I give you the roadmap for
doing this right, let me save you

361
00:27:03,337 --> 00:27:09,067
some time by naming the three ways
I most often see teams get it wrong.

362
00:27:09,867 --> 00:27:13,097
All three feel like reasonable shortcuts.

363
00:27:13,717 --> 00:27:15,987
The first is the cache mirage.

364
00:27:16,587 --> 00:27:21,457
You put a fast-read cache in
front of your batch warehouse,

365
00:27:21,517 --> 00:27:22,797
and you call it real-time.

366
00:27:23,437 --> 00:27:25,267
But it is not real-time.

367
00:27:25,797 --> 00:27:31,317
It is fast access to stale truth,
and that is actually worse than

368
00:27:31,317 --> 00:27:36,997
slow access to stale truth, because
now the agent trusts it completely.

369
00:27:38,057 --> 00:27:44,317
The second is treating this as a cleaner
project, a one-time data quality scrub

370
00:27:44,617 --> 00:27:46,687
with a start date and an end date.

371
00:27:47,257 --> 00:27:49,207
But drift never stops.

372
00:27:49,887 --> 00:27:53,667
The day you declare victory is
the day it starts decaying again.

373
00:27:54,647 --> 00:27:57,407
This is a living service, not a project.

374
00:27:58,127 --> 00:28:00,787
The third is over-merge.

375
00:28:01,577 --> 00:28:06,377
Teams get seduced by the idea
of one perfect golden record,

376
00:28:06,677 --> 00:28:08,657
and they merge too aggressively.

377
00:28:09,547 --> 00:28:10,527
Remember the demo?

378
00:28:10,977 --> 00:28:18,107
For an agent that acts, a false merge is
more dangerous than an honest duplicate.

379
00:28:18,947 --> 00:28:21,587
When in doubt, do not merge.

380
00:28:22,917 --> 00:28:26,047
Avoid those three, and you
are most of the way there.

381
00:28:26,687 --> 00:28:30,437
So here is the way to
do it right in phases

382
00:28:31,437 --> 00:28:36,227
So how do you actually get from where
you are to where the right lane is?

383
00:28:36,877 --> 00:28:40,447
You do not boil the ocean,
you go in four phases.

384
00:28:40,977 --> 00:28:43,057
Phase one, assess.

385
00:28:43,837 --> 00:28:49,697
Audit your current master data for
latency, for drift, for identity gaps,

386
00:28:50,167 --> 00:28:54,887
and find the touch points where an
agent is already acting, because that

387
00:28:54,887 --> 00:28:57,597
is where your risk is concentrated.

388
00:28:57,987 --> 00:28:58,587
Start there.

389
00:28:59,767 --> 00:29:01,837
Phase two is harden.

390
00:29:02,587 --> 00:29:09,727
Bring in immutable records, deterministic
keys, and idempotency, but only on your

391
00:29:09,737 --> 00:29:12,387
most critical entity domains first.

392
00:29:13,637 --> 00:29:15,247
Phase three, stream.

393
00:29:16,027 --> 00:29:20,607
Replace the batch pipelines with
event-driven change data capture

394
00:29:21,247 --> 00:29:23,297
and put your contracts on the bus.

395
00:29:24,307 --> 00:29:26,057
Phase four, operate.

396
00:29:26,867 --> 00:29:32,887
Stand up the drift monitoring, the agent
feedback loops, the contract versioning.

397
00:29:33,657 --> 00:29:38,787
This is where master data stops being
a project and becomes a living service.

398
00:29:39,547 --> 00:29:44,057
The thing I want you to hear in
this is permission to start small.

399
00:29:45,827 --> 00:29:47,717
You do not need to rebuild everything.

400
00:29:48,457 --> 00:29:53,537
You need to start with the entities
your agents already touch Let

401
00:29:53,537 --> 00:29:54,977
me leave you with three things.

402
00:29:55,557 --> 00:29:57,687
If you forget the diagrams, keep these.

403
00:29:59,027 --> 00:30:03,627
One, assume your agents
will act, not advise.

404
00:30:04,457 --> 00:30:10,397
Design your data layer for the moment
of execution, not the moment of a query.

405
00:30:11,947 --> 00:30:17,097
Two, immutability and idempotency
are not optional anymore.

406
00:30:18,387 --> 00:30:22,987
In a world of guaranteed retries and
redelivery, they are the difference

407
00:30:23,007 --> 00:30:28,337
between a system that is correct and
the one that is merely usually correct.

408
00:30:29,497 --> 00:30:34,047
Three, your data contracts
are your agent's API.

409
00:30:34,587 --> 00:30:37,417
Version them, enforce them, own them.

410
00:30:38,707 --> 00:30:43,057
And here is a sentence I would
write on the wall: The data

411
00:30:43,067 --> 00:30:46,097
layer is no longer behind the AI.

412
00:30:46,957 --> 00:30:51,887
It is the thing that decides
whether the AI is safe to act at all

413
00:30:52,887 --> 00:30:54,077
That is the heart of it.

414
00:30:55,357 --> 00:30:59,567
Thank you for watching, and thank you to
the Conf forty-two team for having me.

415
00:31:00,337 --> 00:31:04,467
If any of this was useful or if
you want to argue with me about

416
00:31:04,467 --> 00:31:06,527
it, I would genuinely love that.

417
00:31:07,097 --> 00:31:11,487
Drop your questions in the Conf
forty-two Discord or in the comments,

418
00:31:11,547 --> 00:31:13,957
and I will answer every one of them.

419
00:31:14,657 --> 00:31:21,087
You can al-al-also find me at
my personal website or on X. I'm

420
00:31:21,087 --> 00:31:22,487
also happy to connect on LinkedIn.

421
00:31:24,137 --> 00:31:25,117
That is the talk.

422
00:31:25,237 --> 00:31:26,127
Thank you so much

